module gunbc.falsifier_alert_residue

import std.types { NonEmptyStr, String }
import std.content_hash { Sha256Digest }

data falsifier_alert_residue_purpose_note: String = "ALERT-0. This module DECLARES the falsifier alert's residue and the laws its migration must satisfy. IT MOVES NO BEHAVIOR. The embedded program still runs exactly where it ran before; what changes is that it is now a counted row with a named disposition and a dissolution trigger rather than an undeclared fact of the tree. That ordering is deliberate — a residue that is not declared cannot be shown to have shrunk, and a migration that starts by moving behavior has no baseline to measure against."

data language_normal_form_residue_note: String = "WHAT LANGUAGE-NORMAL-FORM RESIDUE MEANS, stated at the grain that makes it decidable. A .dag corpus that emits foreign artifacts will always contain foreign-language TEXT; that alone is not residue. Residue is foreign text that is in NORMAL FORM for its target language — already a program in that language, authored as such — as opposed to text DERIVED from a modeled structure by an emitter. `bash_build_*` constructors produce shell text and are not residue, because the authority is the Node tree and the text is its projection. A 232-line ECMAScript program pasted into a YAML scalar is residue, because the ECMAScript IS the authority: nothing upstream of it describes what it does, and the only way to change its behavior is to edit ECMAScript. The distinction is exactly the one DESIGN §5 draws between construction and validation, applied to emission: derived text has a single authority, normal-form text is the authority."

type ResidueTarget
  = ECMAScript
  | Bash
  | Yaml

type ResidueRuntime
  = GithubScriptAction
  | GithubActionsRunStep
  | HostShell

data residue_kind_note: String = "ExecutableSourceString is the kind where the residue is a PROGRAM carried as a string value — parsed and executed by a foreign interpreter at runtime, with no modeled structure behind it. It is distinguished from a configuration literal (a string the foreign runtime reads as data) because the remedies differ: a configuration literal wants a typed carrier projected into the same position, while an executable source string wants its BEHAVIOR remodeled and the string deleted. Naming the kind is what makes the disposition derivable rather than chosen per row."

type ResidueKind
  = ExecutableSourceString
  | ConfigurationLiteral

data residue_disposition_note: String = "ReplaceWithLanguageIndependentBehavior is the disposition that says: this residue does NOT become an emitter for its target language. The behavior is remodeled as ordinary typed .dag over typed effects, the foreign program is DELETED, and only a thin foreign-executor wrapper remains — a wrapper that invokes a controller, carrying no business logic of its own. It is deliberately distinct from EmitFromModel, which is the right disposition when the artifact genuinely must BE a program in the target language (a generated shell script, a generated workflow file). Choosing EmitFromModel here would have converted a business-logic migration into a JavaScript-emission project, which is a different and much larger lane, and would have left the same logic maintained in the same language with an extra step in front of it."

type ResidueDisposition
  = ReplaceWithLanguageIndependentBehavior
  | EmitFromModel

type LanguageNormalFormResidue {
  site: NonEmptyStr
  target: ResidueTarget
  runtime: ResidueRuntime
  kind: ResidueKind
  disposition: ResidueDisposition
  dissolves_on: NonEmptyStr
}

data falsifier_alert_embedded_program_residue: LanguageNormalFormResidue = LanguageNormalFormResidue {
  site: ".github/workflows/falsifier-alert.yml",
  target: ECMAScript,
  runtime: GithubScriptAction,
  kind: ExecutableSourceString,
  disposition: ReplaceWithLanguageIndependentBehavior,
  dissolves_on: "the generated typed falsifier-alert workflow cutover — the alert's decision algebra runs as .dag over typed receipt, clock, state and issue operations, and the github-script step carries no program"
}

data falsifier_alert_residue_registry: List<LanguageNormalFormResidue> = [
  falsifier_alert_embedded_program_residue
]

data falsifier_alert_runtime_acquisition_note: String = "THE ALERT CONTROLLER MUST REMAIN AVAILABLE WHEN THE WORKFLOW IT OBSERVES CANNOT BUILD. This is the load-bearing constraint and it eliminates all three obvious acquisition routes, each for its own reason.\n\nBUILD IT IN THE ALERT JOB: fails exactly when the incident IS a build failure. The observer would go dark in the case it most needs to report, and — worse — it would go dark SILENTLY, because a failed build in the alert job looks like an infrastructure blip rather than a suppressed alert. An observer whose availability is correlated with the health of its subject is not an observer.\n\nDOWNLOAD IT FROM THE TRIGGERING RUN: fails when that run died before publishing artifacts, which is a normal way for the observed run to fail. It also makes the observer execute an artifact produced by the run it is judging, so a run that fails in a way that corrupts its own outputs gets to choose what is said about it.\n\nUSE A MUTABLE PATH LIKE /opt/gunbc/bin/gunbc: silently runs whatever is installed. There is no revision in the record, so two alert runs a week apart can behave differently with nothing anywhere saying so, and a bad deploy to that path changes the observer's behavior without any change to this repository.\n\nTHE SHAPE THAT SURVIVES ALL THREE is a CONTENT-ADDRESSED LAST-KNOWN-GOOD bundle: the binary, the exact alert-program source closure it interprets, and a manifest binding both to one controller revision, selected and VERIFIED THROUGH THE MANIFEST. Last-known-good here is deliberate observer independence, not accidental staleness — the observer is pinned to a revision that was known to work, precisely so that breaking the tree cannot break the thing that reports the tree is broken. A versioned path may be the storage realization, but the path is never the authority: the manifest is, and a bundle whose contents do not hash to the manifest refuses rather than running. An exact triggering-run bundle may become an optional FAST PATH later, once trust and identity admission exist to say whose artifact it is — never the only path, and never the default."

type FalsifierAlertRuntime {
  controller_revision: NonEmptyStr
  binary_digest: Sha256Digest
  source_closure_digest: Sha256Digest
}

data falsifier_alert_runtime_verification_note: String = "VERIFICATION IS A REFUSAL, NOT A WARNING. The bundle is admitted only when the binary and the source closure both hash to the digests the manifest names for the selected controller revision. A mismatch on either is a typed, located refusal that stops the alert — it does not fall back to a different bundle, to a mutable path, or to running the binary anyway. The reasoning is DESIGN §5's absorbing-fallback rule at its sharpest: this controller holds issues: write, so an unverified binary is a thing with write access to the repository's issue tracker, and 'proceed as if verification had passed' is the escape hatch that makes the verification decorative. A refusal here means the alert does not run, which is a visible outage of the observer — and a visible outage is strictly better than an invisible compromise of it."

type FalsifierAlertRuntimeAdmission
  = RuntimeAdmitted { runtime: FalsifierAlertRuntime }
  | RuntimeManifestAbsent { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | RuntimeBinaryDigestMismatch { expected: Sha256Digest, observed: Sha256Digest }
  | RuntimeSourceClosureDigestMismatch { expected: Sha256Digest, observed: Sha256Digest }

data falsifier_alert_runtime_scaffold_note: String = "gunbc.cli_invoke gunbc_run_shell assumes $ROOT/target/release/gunbc. That is an EMISSION SCAFFOLD for steps that run inside a checkout which has just built the binary — it is not, and must not become, the acquisition authority for this alert. Reusing it here would silently reintroduce the build-it-in-the-job route rejected above, because it presumes exactly the successful build whose absence is the incident. The acquisition authority is the manifest; that this module says so explicitly is what keeps a later author from reaching for the nearest existing helper."
